  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...An anomalously strong upper level trough\namplifies over the eastern US today, with a potent shortwave \ndives southeast through VA and the Carolinas. A strong LLJ \nahead of this feature should lead to increasing moisture \nadvection, with PWATs once again climbing back above 2\". Meanwhile,\nheating of a moist boundary layer should support moderate \ninstability, with MLCAPE rising into the 1500-2500 j/kg range. \nAloft, a notable upper jet max is forecast to overspread the \ninstability axis, supporting deep layer shear of 30-45kt. While \ninstability will be moderate, the shear/instability/forcing \ncombo is notable for this time of year. This combination\nsupports the threat for severe weather, with modest support \nfrom ensemble, analog, and machine learning guidance. The main \npotential limiting factors are timing and potential subsidence, \nwith area remaining capped through much of today. Some guidance\nsuggests convection may not reach ENC until after sunset. \nDespite the potential for a gradually stabilizing boundary \nlayer, if strong large-scale forcing is realized, it should help\nsupport deep convection well past the typical diurnal max in \nconvective intensity this time of year. Storm mode could be a \nbit messy, with combination of supercells early ahead of more \norganized multicell line segments later. Given the potential \namount of forcing present, it appears likely that a MCS will \nmove through the eastern Carolinas and VA during the evening and\nearly overnight hours posing a risk of damaging winds. Low- \nlevel SRH around 100-200m2/s2 may support a few rotating \nupdrafts capable of hail and tornados. However, those latter 2 \nhazards should be lower compared to the wind risk. While \nconvection looks to be fairly progressive, heavy rain in a high \nPWAT airmass could pose a localized flood threat, esp for areas\nthat have received heavy rain over the last few days (Hyde \ncounty east through the Outer Banks).\n\nAhead of the convection, hot and humid conditions are expected,\nwith heat indices rising to 100-105 degrees for portions of the\narea. Still looks too marginal for heat advisory at this time. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cold front will push offshore early Wed \nmorning, with sfc high pressure building in from the west while \ntroughing continues aloft. This will keep temps seasonable and \nmostly dry through late week. The next frontal system will \nimpact the area this weekend into early next week, with \nincreasing precip chances.",
